    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§265.45 & 265.50, Pen L (as proposed in S.2450-A & A.2686-A)",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6360\n\nSPONSOR: KRUEGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the penal law, in relation to storage\nof firearms\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to clarify certain authorized situations in\nwhich an individual is not violating the requirement to safely store\nfirearms, rifles and shotguns in the presence of a child.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of this bill makes a clarifying amendment to Penal Law §\n265.45 (Failure to safely store rifles, shotguns, and firearms in the\nfirst degree).\n\nSection two of this bill makes a clarifying amendment to Penal Law §\n265.50 (Failure to safely store rifles, shotguns, and firearms in the\nsecond degree).\n\nSection three of this bill is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nRequiring the safe storage of firearms, rifles and shotguns is an impor-\ntant measure in preventing unnecessary tragedies throughout New York\nState. However, such requirement was not intended to prevent young\npeople from having access to supervised use of firearms, rifles and\nshotguns as part of a school or other educational program, or while\nsupervised by a parent or guardian a person designated by a parent or\nguardian, as is already authorized by law. This bill would clarify that\nallowing a person less than sixteen years of age access to a gun for\nlawful use as already permitted under the penal law would not be a\nviolation of the requirement to keep such firearm, rifle or shotgun\nsafely stored.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone noted.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill would take effect in the same manner as S.2450a of 2019.\n",
